x86_64-windows  clang compiles this against Microsoft's own <initializer_list>, whose default constructor it emits as `??0?$initializer_list@H@std@@QEAA@XZ`; cxx1's <initializer_list> has the same constructor and emits nothing for it, nothing here calling it. A header difference, not a mangling one - every name both emit agrees. x86_64-linux and arm64-darwin are compared on the Mac: this box's clang has no headers for those targets.
x86_64-linux  cxx1 emits the C1 and D1 forms of constructors and destructors defined inside their class where clang emits only the C2 a use asks for - the difference implicit-move.nonames records. Measured on the Windows box with clang -target x86_64-linux-gnu over cxx1's own headers, every other name agreeing (this box's names.sh cannot, its clang having no headers for that target).
arm64-darwin  Apple's libc++ tags its own initializer_list members with an ABI tag (begin`B9nqe220106`Ev and the like) that cxx1's <initializer_list> does not carry - a header difference; every name net of the tag agrees; and libc++'s header defines the default constructor clang emits, which cxx1's does not need.
